On Thursday, January 3, 2013 2:10:22 AM UTC-5, Noah wrote:

> Ben,
>

> That makes sense.  Currently I am doing it the *slow* way.
>
> My understanding was that each covariate was a vector in stan.  How can I combine them all into a single matrix so that I may then use matrix algebra?  Is there an example online somewhere that I can study?

Each covariate *can* be a vector but need not be. And as you discovered, doing so can lead to computational problems.

The easiest thing to do is to write the matrix to a file as a matrix and then read it as a matrix in the data {} block. There are examples of this in the manual. If you are using RStan, there is a function called stan_rdump() that facilitates this. Just make a data.frame of covariates called X (in addition to a y vector) and do something like:

stan_rdump(c("y", X"), file = "my_data.R")

If you are not using R, then let us know and we can probably provide suggestions.

If all else fails, you could read the data in as vectors and use a transformed data {} block to glue them together into a matrix, roughly:

data {
  int<lower=1> N;
  int<lower=1> K;

  ...
}
transformed data {
  real mean_y;

  matrix[N,K] X;
  mean_y <- mean(y);
  for(i in 1:N) {
    X[i,1] <- x_var_1[i];
    X[i,2] <- x_var_2[i];
    ...
    X[i,200] <- x_var_200[i];
  }
}
parameters {
  real intercept_shift;
  vector[K] betas;

  real<lower=0,upper=1> sigma_betas;
  real<lower=0,upper=1> sigma_y;
}
model {
  betas ~ normal(0,sigma_betas);

  y ~ normal(mean_y + intercept_shift + X * beta, sigma_y);
}

Ben

On Friday, January 4, 2013 11:45:24 AM UTC-5, Marcel Falkiewicz wrote:
>  I'd like to ask if you could implement Ex-Gaussian distribution in STAN. The distribution is described in http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponentially_modified_Gaussian_distribution . Since my mathematical skills are non-existent, I would be greatful for help :)

Thanks for the suggestion. I imagine it will be implemented in Stan eventually, which would make it possible to optimize the calculation of the derivatives a bit. In the meantime, it is easy enough to use this (log) density in Stan with something like:

data {
  int<lower=1> N;
  real y[N];
}
transformed data {
  real sum_y;
  sum_y <- sum(y);
}
parameters {
  real mu;
  real<lower=0> sigma;
  real<lower=0> lambda;
}
model {
  real half_lambda;
  real sigma2;
  real sqrt_2;
  real summands[N];

  half_lambda <- 0.5 * lambda;
  sigma2 <- square(sigma);
  sqrt_2 <- sqrt(2.0);

  // log-likelihood
  lp <- lp + log(half_lambda) + half_lambda * (2 * mu + lambda * sigma2);
  lp <- lp - lambda * sum_y;
  for(n in 1:N) {
    summands[i] <- log(erfc( (mu + lambda * sigma2 - x[n]) / (sqrt_2 * sigma) ));
  }
  lp <- lp + sum(summands);
  // optional priors on parameters
}

Bah, all my instances of lp should have two trailing underscores, as in lp__ . And you could calculate the square root of 2 in the transformed data block rather than the model block.

Ben

